WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:03.703
The moment that I was introduced
to K-pop was on

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:06.840
Saturday Night Live
in April 2019,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.377
when the group BTS or Bangtan
Sonyeondan appeared.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.114 size:88% position:44% line:0%
Being Asian American myself,
I hadn't realized how much

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:17.917 size:81% position:41% line:0%
I had missed seeing people
that look like me onscreen

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.220
on a show that I had watched
since I was a kid.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.591
There's a long history of
Asian American men in the media

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.693
either
being just completely invisible

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:31.031
or being deemed as not worthy
of romantic relationships.

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:34.067
So from a sociological
point of view, that itself

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.402
seemed interesting.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:38.471
There are also articles
about how Asian Americans,

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:42.275
who always felt like they were
somehow ashamed or felt like

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:46.279
they weren't cool, that somehow
K-pop elevated their status.

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:49.949
My collaborator,
who is Korean, also said

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:53.219
that people recognize
his name as being Korean,

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.221
and suddenly
being Korean was cool.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.389
When I was a kid,

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:58.825
if you had an ethnic name,
an Asian ethnic name,

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.727
people tried really hard
to change it.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.596
The most popular person on
TikTok is a guy named Jungkook,

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.932
like he didn't have
to anglicize his name.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:09.102
And so I never thought this
would happen in my lifetime.

